.TH ICED\-LIBADWAITA\-DOCK 3 "" "iced-libadwaita 0.1.0" "iced-libadwaita Manual"
.SH NAME
dock \- a surface docked against an edge of a base: a footer, a banner, a drawer, a bottom sheet (AdwBottomSheet)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
Dock::new(base: impl Into<Element<'a, M>>, surface: impl Into<Element<'a, M>>)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBDock\fR: a surface docked against an edge of a base: a footer, a banner, a drawer, a bottom sheet (\fBAdwBottomSheet\fR). One widget; you define its size, its length and its place yourself:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBside\fR (\fBSide\fR): the edge it is docked to (default the bottom).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsize\fR (\fBDockSize\fR): how far it reaches across the edge: \fBAuto\fR (its own), \fBFixed(px)\fR, a \fBFraction\fR of the base, or \fBFullScreen\fR: the whole base, every border flush.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlength\fR (\fBDockSize\fR) and \fBalign\fR (\fBAlign\fR): how long it is along the edge (default the whole edge) and, when shorter, where along it: the start, the center or the end. This is the flex and position of a floating panel.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmargin\fR: how far it stands off the edge it is docked to, and off that edge only. \fB0\fR (the default) is \fBflush\fR with the edge; a margin lifts it, and its corners on that edge round.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmode\fR (\fBDockMode\fR): \fBPush\fR puts it in flow: it slides in and the base gives up exactly the room it takes, like a sidebar beside the content (\fBAdaptiveSplit\fR and \fBprimitives::dock\fR, the same geometry as \fBNavigationSplitView\fR and its drawer); \fBOverlay\fR floats it over the base on an \fBOverlay\fR: the base is faded and disabled, the surface slides in from its edge, and a press outside sends \fBon_outside\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBscope\fR (\fBDockScope\fR): \fBContent\fR docks to the base as given (a pane, the content area); \fBWindow\fR docks to the whole window, so the corners that touch the window's corners take its radius (\fBwindow_radius\fR).
.PP
A pushed dock takes the whole edge (length, align, margin and border are the overlay's).
.PP
A hairline \fBborder\fR (the gallery previews' kind) outlines the surface on the sides that are not flush with the base: a docked surface standing off its edge is outlined all around, a flush one only where it meets the content; \fBFullScreen\fR has none. \fBborder(false)\fR removes it.
.PP
\fBFullScreen\fR covers the base and ignores margin: all its borders are flush (square, or the window's radius for a window dock). The base decides the area: wrap the window's root for a window dock, a pane for a content one. \fBopen\fR shows or hides it without moving the base in the widget tree.
.PP
.nf
.RS
Dock::new(content, sheet)
    .open(self.open)
    .side(Side::Bottom)
    .size(DockSize::Fixed(240.0))
    .length(DockSize::Fixed(480.0))      // 480 wide ...
    .align(Align::Center)                // ... centered on the edge
    .margin(12.0)                        // floating 12 px off the bottom
    .mode(DockMode::Overlay)
    .on_outside(Message::Close)
.RE
.fi
